From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mason Chang <mason-cw.chang@mediatek.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:52:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: free what mtk_rx_alloc() got when
 it fails

If mtk_create_page_pool() fails, ring->data allocated via kcalloc()
was not freed before returning the error, causing a memory leak.

The same happens when the coherent descriptor ring can't be allocated:
mtk_rx_clean() only frees ring->data when the ring has descriptors.
And until the loop that adds the buffers is done, ring->dma_size is
still 0 on a first open, so after a failure in that loop
mtk_rx_clean() neither returns the buffers already added (the
page_pool then can't be destroyed) nor frees the coherent ring at its
size.

Free ring->data in mtk_rx_clean() whether or not the ring has
descriptors, and set ring->dma_size before the buffers are added.

[pesa1234 999-9923, from MediaTek's SDK. Ported to OpenWrt 6.12 for
 MilkFlint by OddBlueDog: fixed in mtk_rx_clean() instead of at the
 page_pool error, to cover the other two failure paths as well.]

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c | 15 ++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
index 85f17bd..d1f2eb1 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -3072,6 +3072,12 @@ static int mtk_rx_alloc(struct mtk_eth *eth, int ring_no, int rx_flag)
 	if (!ring->dma)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
+	/* Set before the buffers are added, so that mtk_rx_clean() returns
+	 * the ones added before a failure, and frees the coherent ring at
+	 * its size.
+	 */
+	ring->dma_size = rx_dma_size;
+
 	for (i = 0; i < rx_dma_size; i++) {
 		struct mtk_rx_dma_v2 *rxd;
 		dma_addr_t dma_addr;
@@ -3122,7 +3128,6 @@ static int mtk_rx_alloc(struct mtk_eth *eth, int ring_no, int rx_flag)
 		}
 	}
 
-	ring->dma_size = rx_dma_size;
 	ring->calc_idx_update = false;
 	ring->calc_idx = rx_dma_size - 1;
 	if (rx_flag == MTK_RX_FLAGS_QDMA)
@@ -3180,10 +3185,14 @@ static void mtk_rx_clean(struct mtk_eth *eth, struct mtk_rx_ring *ring, bool in_
 					 ring->buf_size, DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
 			mtk_rx_put_buff(ring, ring->data[i], false);
 		}
-		kfree(ring->data);
-		ring->data = NULL;
 	}
 
+	/* mtk_rx_alloc() can fail after allocating ring->data but before
+	 * the ring has its descriptors: free it either way.
+	 */
+	kfree(ring->data);
+	ring->data = NULL;
+
 	if (!in_sram && ring->dma) {
 		dma_free_coherent(eth->dma_dev,
 				  RX_DESC_OFS(eth, ring->dma_size),
